.TH DROP 1 2026-10-22 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
drop, cdrop, pdrop \- run a command without administrator privileges
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B drop
.RB [[ \-\- ]
.I command
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B drop
.B \-s
.I shell
.br
.B drop
.RB [ \-s
.IR shell ]
.B \-c
.I string
.RB [[ \-\- ]
.IR argument ...]
.br
.B cdrop
.RB [[ \-\- ]
.I command
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B cdrop \-c
.I string
.RB [[ \-\- ]
.IR argument ...]
.br
.B pdrop
.RB [[ \-\- ]
.I command
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B pdrop \-c
.I string
.RB [[ \-\- ]
.IR argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR drop ,
.BR cdrop ,
and
.B pdrop
run a command with the rights of a standard user.
They are meant to be run from an elevated process, one that has
administrator rights through User Account Control (UAC), such as a shell
started by
.BR su (1).
The command runs as the same user, in the same console, with the same
standard input, output, and error, environment, and current directory.
These programs are busybox applets; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Each program builds a new access token for the command from its own
token.
In the new token the Administrators group is disabled, the privileges
are those Windows gives a standard user, and the integrity level is
medium.
This is the Windows "Normal User" software restriction level.
.PP
With a
.I command
operand, all three programs run that command with the remaining operands
as its arguments.
A
.I command
that names a busybox applet runs that applet from the running
.IR busybox.exe ,
even when
.B PATH
has a program of the same name, unless
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
says otherwise.
A
.I command
that contains a slash, a backslash, or a drive letter is the path of a
file.
Any other
.I command
is searched for in
.BR PATH .
For a path and in the search, if the name is not an executable file,
the suffixes
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
and
.B .cmd
are tried in that order, unless the name already ends with one of them
or with a period.
.PP
With no
.I command
operand, or with
.BR \-c ,
the programs differ in the shell they run:
.TP
.B drop
runs the kit's
.BR sh (1)
from the running
.IR busybox.exe ,
or the program named by
.BR \-s .
.TP
.B cdrop
runs
.BR cmd.exe ,
found in
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B pdrop
runs Windows PowerShell,
.BR powershell.exe ,
found in
.BR PATH .
.PP
Without
.BR \-c ,
the shell is interactive.
With
.BR \-c ,
the shell runs
.I string
and exits, and the operands follow
.I string
as arguments.
In
.BR sh (1),
the first of them is
.BR $0 .
.PP
Each program waits for the command to exit, and exits with its status.
The program reads no input and writes no output of its own, apart from
error
messages and
.BR \-\-help .
.SH OPTIONS
Options may follow operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set, so an option meant for the command is taken as an option of the
program.
Put
.B \-\-
before the command to end the options, as in
.BR "drop \-\- ls \-l" .
An unknown option or an option without its argument is a usage error:
the program prints a usage message on standard error and exits with
status 1.
.TP
.BI "\-c " string
Run
.I string
as a command in the shell.
The shell is given
.B \-c
.IR string ,
or
.B /c
.I string
for
.BR cmd.exe .
The operands follow as arguments.
.TP
.BI "\-s " shell
.RB ( drop
only.)
Run
.I shell
instead of
.BR sh (1).
.I shell
is the path of a program, with forward slashes or backslashes.
It is not searched for in
.BR PATH :
a name without a directory is looked for in the current directory, with
the suffixes listed above.
When the last component of
.I shell
is
.B cmd.exe
in any case,
.B \-c
passes
.B /c
instead.
When
.I shell
is not found,
.B drop
exits with status 127.
.B \-s
is ignored when there is a
.I command
operand and no
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and exit
with status 0.
Any further arguments are ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Where
.IR command ,
.BR cmd.exe ,
and
.B powershell.exe
are searched for.
.TP
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
Which names run an applet and which run a program from
.BR PATH ;
see
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.BR USER ", " LOGNAME
Each is set for the command to the name of the user, with spaces
replaced by underscores, when it is unset or is
.BR root .
Otherwise it is passed on unchanged.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
The C library's option parser reads it.
When it is set, even to an empty value, options are recognized only
before the first operand.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 1
A usage error, a failure to build the new token, or a failure to read
the status of the command.
Also a status of 1 from the command.
.TP
.B 126
The command was found but could not be started.
.TP
.B 127
The command, the
.B \-s
shell,
.BR cmd.exe ,
or
.B powershell.exe
was not found.
.PP
Otherwise the exit status is that of the command, any value from 0 to
255.
A command that ended as if by a signal has status 128 plus the signal
number, such as 139 for an access violation and 130 for Ctrl+C.
Otherwise the status is the low byte of the Windows exit code, or 255
for a code that is a nonzero multiple of 256.
Such a code above 255 is also reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
drop: \fImessage\fR Error 0x\fIcode\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
with the code in hexadecimal, after the Windows description of it when
there is one.
The other errors are reported as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
drop: can\(aqt find \(aq\fIname\fR\(aq
drop: can\(aqt execute \(aq\fIpath\fR\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
with the name of the program in place of
.BR drop .
A failure to build the new token has no message.
.SH EXAMPLES
From an elevated shell, open a shell without administrator rights in the
same console:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ drop
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build a project without administrator rights.
The
.B \-\-
keeps
.B \-j4
away from
.BR drop :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ drop \-\- make \-j4
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a shell command, passing a file name as
.BR $0 :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ drop \-c \(aqls \-l "$0"\(aq notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Open a
.B cmd.exe
prompt without administrator rights:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cdrop
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Windows still reports the dropped process as elevated.
To tell the difference, busybox tests whether the Administrators group
is enabled, so in a dropped process
.BR id (1)
does not print
.BR root ,
and
.BR su (1)
refuses to run from a dropped process, because it cannot raise the
privilege again.
.PP
When the program is not elevated it works the same way.
The command then runs with no more rights than the program has, at
medium integrity.
If Windows refuses to build the new token or to set its integrity
level, the program exits with status 1 without a message.
Integrity levels exist only on Windows Vista and later, so on Windows XP
the programs always fail this way.
.PP
On Ctrl+C or Ctrl+Break, a command that shares the console handles the
event itself.
A command that does not, such as a GUI program, is terminated.
The program itself keeps waiting for the command to exit.
.PP
Each argument is quoted by the rules of the Microsoft C runtime.
.B cmd.exe
and PowerShell parse their command lines by their own rules, so a
.I string
that contains double quotes may reach them changed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR id (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR su (1)
